# Copy to .env beside this file. That .env is git-ignored and never committed.
#
#   cp deploy/compose/.env.example deploy/compose/.env
#
# Compose reads .env from this directory - the one holding the compose file -
# rather than from wherever you run the command, so a .env in the repository
# root is silently ignored. Nothing warns you about that, which is why it is the
# first line of this file.
#
# Three kinds of value end up here, and they behave differently:
#
#   - Keys the compose file does not mention (every PANORAMA_OIDC_* below) are
#     passed straight into the container.
#   - Keys it substitutes, like PANORAMA_PORT, feed the substitution.
#   - Keys it sets literally, like PANORAMA_DATABASE_DRIVER, are NOT overridable
#     here. The compose file stays the authority on how the container is wired.
#
# Every value is optional. With no .env at all the deployment still starts, on
# port 8088, with single sign-on off.

# The host port. On its own it also decides the base URL below.
PANORAMA_PORT=8088

# Which found price table the build embeds: rates for models the AWS Price List
# API does not publish, such as GPT-5.x on Bedrock. `latest` fetches the one
# attached to the latest release on GitHub; a tag such as v1.4.0 fetches that
# release's, for a build that does not change under you; `none` embeds nothing,
# and those models are counted as unpriced. Only read when the image is built.
# PANORAMA_FOUND_PRICES=latest

# The address a browser reaches the console at. It is also the redirect URI your
# identity provider must have registered, and the URL engineers point a tool at -
# run `panorama sso presets` to see the exact URI it produces.
#
# Defaults to http://localhost:<PANORAMA_PORT>, which is right on your own
# machine and wrong everywhere else. On a server - an EC2 instance, a VM, behind
# a load balancer - set it to the address people actually use, including the
# scheme and any port.
#PANORAMA_SERVER_BASE_URL=https://panorama.your-company.internal

# The development stack (`make dev`) reads this same file and publishes two more
# ports. Change them if something else on this machine holds one - notably the
# evaluation stack above, which also wants 8088, so the two cannot run at once
# on their defaults.
#PANORAMA_CONSOLE_PORT=5173
#PANORAMA_POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# Single sign-on. Validate the issuer before you restart anything:
#
#   panorama sso probe -preset entra-id -issuer https://…
#
# and confirm it afterwards with `panorama sso doctor`.
#PANORAMA_OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0
#PANORAMA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
#PANORAMA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The token claim carrying group membership. It is not the same word at every
# provider - Amazon Cognito calls it cognito:groups, and Auth0 requires a
# namespaced URI. `panorama sso presets` lists what each one uses.
#PANORAMA_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=groups

# Limits sign-in to accounts in these domains, comma-separated. Leave it unset
# for a provider whose issuer names your own tenant. Required for Google
# Workspace, whose issuer signs in every Google account there is - Panorama
# refuses to start on it without this.
#PANORAMA_OIDC_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com

# Model providers. Credentials come from the AWS default chain; mount ~/.aws by
# uncommenting the volume in docker-compose.yml, or set AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and
# friends here.
#
# AWS_REGION seeds the bedrock provider on the first start, and only then. After
# that each provider's region is set in the console, under Settings, Model
# providers - bedrock-mantle is turned on there too, in whatever region you
# choose - and changing AWS_REGION here changes nothing but a startup warning.
#AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#AWS_PROFILE=

# How often Panorama asks each provider what this account can invoke. That
# answer is what the console's model catalogue is built from. Hours rather than
# minutes; negative disables it.
#PANORAMA_PROVIDERS_DISCOVERY_INTERVAL=6h

# Beta feature flags to drop before a request reaches Bedrock, seeded onto the
# bedrock provider on the first start.
#
# Bedrock fails a whole request on one flag it does not recognise, and coding
# agents ship new ones faster than Bedrock adopts them. After the first start
# the list is corrected in the console, under the provider's model tables. Leave
# this unset to use the built-in list, which is the usual case.
#PANORAMA_BEDROCK_UNSUPPORTED_BETAS=prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05,advisor-tool-2026-03-01,redact-thinking-2026-02-12,prompt-caching-2024-07-31

# Which models engineers may ask for is NOT set here either. An administrator
# chooses them in the console - from what the providers say this account can
# actually invoke - and they are stored in the database, so changing one needs no
# redeploy.


# The key that protects credentials an administrator stores from the console -
# today the GitHub connection, under Integrations.
#
# Any passphrase of 16 characters or more works; the key is derived from it. Or
# mint a full-strength one, which is used exactly as given rather than derived
# from:
#
#   panorama admin generate-key
#
# Optional, and the deployment starts and runs without it. What it changes is
# one screen: with no key, saving a GitHub credential is refused and says so.
# Everything else - the gateway, budgets, attribution, the console - is
# unaffected.
#
# It stays here while what it protects lives in the database, and that split is
# the point: a dump, a replica or a backup carries no working credential. Keep
# it somewhere you will still have it - lose it and each credential has to be
# entered again.
#
# Editing this value is not a rotation. Anything already stored was sealed with
# the old key and will not open with the new one, so each credential has to be
# entered again in the console.
#PANORAMA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Trying Panorama on your own laptop, with no identity provider.
#
# Naming an account here adds "Sign in as the development account" to the
# console's sign-in screen: one click, an administrator session, no single
# sign-on. It is what the Quickstart uses, because everything worth looking at -
# routing, analytics, My setup - is behind a sign-in.
#
# It is for a laptop and nothing else, and the server enforces that rather than
# trusting this comment: with it set, Panorama refuses to start unless
# PANORAMA_SERVER_BASE_URL is localhost, 127.0.0.1 or ::1. The route does not
# exist at all when this is unset, and every use is logged at WARN. Never set it
# on a deployment other people can reach - connect single sign-on instead.
#PANORAMA_DEV_SIGN_IN_AS=you@example.com

# debug logs every request. info is the default.
#PANORAMA_LOG_LEVEL=info
